# Performance rules

The hot path is the frame path (`docs/design.ja.md` §2.10, §11.5): input dispatch, `Batch` flush, invalidation, materialization, layout, DisplayList and HitTestList build, upload, present. It also covers pointer move and hover handling. Construction of nodes, styles, and resources may allocate; the steady-state frame does not.

These rules come from the shape of Cysharp's R3, ZLinq, ZString, MemoryPack, and MagicOnion (read in `.repos/`), applied to this framework. A claim that a rule was followed is a measurement, not a reading of the code (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`).

## Allocation

- The frame path does not allocate in steady state. The Zero-Alloc tests (design §11.3) assert it; an allowed exception needs an Issue and a design entry.
- No LINQ, `params` arrays, `string` concatenation or interpolation into `string`, boxing (`object` parameters, non-generic interfaces on structs, `Enum.HasFlag` on old paths, `foreach` over an interface), or lambdas that capture on the frame path. A `static` lambda is fine: its delegate is cached.
- `foreach` over a `List<T>` field goes through `CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan`; over a `Dictionary` it uses the struct enumerator directly, never `IEnumerable<T>`.
- A cache or pool that grows on demand has a hard upper bound and a counter of the requests it refused at the bound. Its steady-state limit is not enough: in S8b the glyph atlas added pages while every plot was in use in the frame, and a caller that never started a new frame would have added pages without end (`GlyphAtlas.PageLimit`, `RefusedCount`).
- A pool's demand follows what is held at once, not what a frame asks for in all: a user gives an item back when it is done with it, and a later user of the same frame takes it. R4c's first layer pool gave each layer of a frame a texture of its own, so 100 sibling layers of 330 KB reached the 32 MB limit and the rest were refused; the review found it. A GPU resource that a draw has just read is not drawn into by the next draw: that waits for the read. Two resources take turns. In R4c, giving a layer's texture to the next sibling at once made 50 layers take 685 to 733 µs of GPU against 657 to 671 µs with a texture each; with one layer between, two textures took turns and took 510 to 520 µs (PR #264, `LayerPool.Spacing`).
- A resource the frame path makes when it needs one (a layer's texture) goes to the path of degradation when the device has no memory for it (`E_OUTOFMEMORY`), as when the pool is at its limit; it does not throw in the middle of writing a frame. A lost device and other failures still throw. A test replaces the function that makes the resource to fail (`LayerPool.MakeTexture`; PR #264's review).
- Buffers that live across frames are owned and reused: reset the used length, grow only when full (`DisplayListBuffer`, design §5.0.1). Short-lived scratch comes from `ArrayPool<T>.Shared` or `stackalloc` (fixed small size only), and is returned in a `finally`. Scratch for runs that nest (a flex container's run and its items' runs) comes from a per-thread `ScratchStack<T>`, given back in a `finally`: with `ArrayPool<T>.Shared`, which keeps one array of a size on a thread, a nested container's array goes to the pool's shared stacks, and a `Return` in the steady state allocated now and then on the runners (4,048 bytes in one call, Issue #92). Clear a returned array only when `RuntimeHelpers.IsReferenceOrContainsReferences<T>()`.
- A `[ThreadStatic]` scratch buffer may back a builder that is disposed before the method returns (ZString, MagicOnion). In debug builds the rent takes the buffer out of the slot, so nested use throws instead of corrupting it.
- Text that changes every frame uses `Text(TextBuilder)` and writes into the node's own buffer (design §2.13), not `Text(Func<string>)`. Whether a text update joins the Zero-Alloc scenarios is decided after S8 measures shaping.
- Static data is a `ReadOnlySpan<byte>` (read wider values with `BinaryPrimitives`). A `ReadOnlySpan<ushort>` or `ReadOnlySpan<uint>` of constants points into the executable's data only in Release: a Debug build makes an array at each read, and in T1a the Zero-Alloc test of the line breaker failed in Debug alone, with 19,008 bytes a run (PR #285).
- A step added to a path that runs whenever text changes (`FontFallback.Split`, a measure function) is timed on that path in the PR, before and after. In T1a `Split` walked every grapheme cluster of the text to check one run's end, about 24 µs a screen, and no figure covered it; the review found it (PR #285).
- An `async` API on a repeated path returns `ValueTask` backed by a pooled `IValueTaskSource` (`ManualResetValueTaskSourceCore<T>`), not a new `Task` each time.

## Order of work and conversions

- Work that can be refused is done after it is accepted. T9's rasterizer scaled a PNG glyph in `Measure`, so it scaled glyphs the atlas then refused as larger than a plot; it now scales in `Rasterize`, into the atlas (PR #212 review).
- An unsigned integer becomes a `float` through `int` (`(float)(int)value`) where the value fits: x64 has no instruction from an unsigned integer to a float. In T9's scaler, scaling 136 x 128 to 15 x 15 went from 53 µs to 38 µs (PR #212, found in a profile).

## Collections of subscribers and handles

- A subscriber list that changes during notification is an intrusive linked list whose node is the subscription itself (R3 `Subject`): no separate `IDisposable` per subscription, O(1) removal, and a version stamp so nodes added during a notification are skipped without copying the list.
- An add/remove set keyed by slot uses a free list: removal nulls the slot, the index is the removal key, and the next add reuses the first empty slot (R3 `FreeListCore`). Do not use `ImmutableArray` or copy-on-write arrays for lists that change at run time.
- A fixed small number of items is held in fields, not an array (R3 `Disposable.Combine` up to eight).

## Dispatch and generics

- No interface or virtual dispatch per element on the frame path. Dispatch once per call (a `switch` on a type tag, a sealed class hierarchy matched once), or use a generic method constrained to a struct (`where T : struct, IFoo`) so the JIT and NativeAOT specialize it.
- A feature that most frames do not use gets a copy of the loop without it: a struct with a `static abstract` flag (`IGroupMode.Groups` in `D3D11Renderer.WriteInstances<TMode>`), chosen once per call. Then the copy's functions are compared with main's, instruction by instruction (`dumpbin /disasm`, as in `bench.mdc`). In R4c the copy without groups was still 4 to 7 µs slower in the curves scene until three things were found that way: the native compiler did not inline small generic helpers (`Faded<NoGroups>`, `WriteGlyphRun`), a command copied into a local called `Memmove` per curve, and values kept across a call cost four more saved XMM registers (25 more instructions in `WriteCurve`) (PR #264).
- A check added to each node of a route (a handler's call, a default action) reads what it needs in the read the loop makes of the node already, through a helper marked `AggressiveInlining`; a check that the route does not change (which key it is) is decided once before the loop, and a lookup of a side table (a node's scroll state) is made once per call. K1b's first version read each node twice before calling its handlers, did not inline the helper, tested at each node whether the key scrolls, and looked up the scroll state three times a wheel: every delivery took 10 to 40 % longer than main's, and a 50-deep move about 0.4 µs (PR #360).
- Per-type data is a `static class Cache<T>` field, filled by generated code or a static constructor without reflection, never a `Dictionary<Type, ...>` lookup on the frame path. JIT-constant checks (`typeof(T) == typeof(int)`, `RuntimeHelpers.IsReferenceOrContainsReferences<T>()`) choose fast paths at no cost.
- A struct walker over a tree (`ITraverser`-style, ZLinq) iterates the arena without allocating; its explicit stack comes from a pool and is returned on `Dispose`.
- Iteration over a sequence the framework owns uses a single `TryGetNext(out T)` call per element rather than `MoveNext` + `Current` (ZLinq).

## Fast path and slow path

- Throwing lives in a separate method marked `[DoesNotReturn]` (and `[StackTraceHidden]` when the frame is noise), so the caller stays small enough to inline. Throw helpers in this repository are `ThrowHelper.*`.
- Rare work (growing a buffer, requesting a new segment) lives in a `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)]` method; the common check stays inline (MemoryPack `GetSpanReference` / `RequestNewBuffer`).
- A pixel shader on the frame path does not branch on a value of the pixel or of the instance; it chooses with a conditional value (`a ? b : c` on values both computed). In R4a the rounded rectangle's distance chose a corner with `if`, and the GPU time of the shapes scene (`frames --shapes`) was 6 to 10 % longer than without a branch, even with a branch that every pixel of an instance took the same way, and with `[branch]`. A branch whose other side was emptied was as slow, so the branch itself was the cost; the formula without a branch took two arcs and was 5 % over the formula it replaced (PR #248, `docs/benchmarks/r4a-shapes.md`).
- A variant of a shader is the same source compiled with a macro (`#ifdef FADED`), and the variant without the macro is checked to be the shader's machine code before the change (`D3DDisassemble`). A `bool` parameter folded to `false` was not the same: the compiler ordered the instructions differently, and `frames --shapes` took about 2 % more GPU (R4c, PR #264).
- A new kind of primitive gets a pixel shader of its own, and its values a stream of their own, not fields in a record the frame path already writes. Code in a shader slows every pixel it draws even where no pixel takes it: in R4b the lines, ellipses, and arcs inside the rectangles' shader made the GPU time of `frames` (rectangles and one line of text) 3 to 4 µs longer and that of `frames --shapes` 3 %; in a shader of their own, both were main's again, and the curves scene was 23 µs faster. Their fields in `ShapeRecord` made the shapes scene's display list stage 3 µs longer, as each shape cleared and wrote the larger record (PR #256, `docs/benchmarks/r4b-curves.md`).
- `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]` only where a measurement shows an effect, and the PR says which.
- A writer over a buffer may be a `ref struct` holding a `ref` field and a remaining length instead of a `Span` field (MemoryPack `MemoryPackWriter`). Bounds are checked once per reservation, not per byte.

## Memory layout

- Data read together is stored together. The arena stores `UiNode` structs and is walked by index; GPU-bound data is SoA and copied with `MemoryMarshal.AsBytes` (design §5.0.1).
- Unmanaged structs that cross to native code or the GPU have `[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]`; others may use `LayoutKind.Auto`.
- A field added to a struct on the frame path goes where the struct has padding, with `Unsafe.SizeOf` read before and after. In L5 a 4-byte field before the measurement cache's entries made it 464 bytes a node instead of 456 and the first layout of 10,101 nodes 7 % slower; after the flags it fit the padding (PR #112). A field that fits no padding goes into an array of its own, indexed as the struct's array is: V3b's remembered overflow (9 bytes) made `MeasureCache` 592 bytes instead of 576 wherever it went, read with `Unsafe.SizeOf` in a scratch test before anything was measured, and lives in a 12-byte array beside it (PR #377).
- The size of a class's instance is measured by what making one allocates (`GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread` before and after); `Unsafe.SizeOf<T>` of a class is the size of a reference, 8 bytes. V1c's design said `NodeVisual` and `PseudoStyles` would be measured with `Unsafe.SizeOf`, and writing an answer to its review found it (PR #319).
- A function that every node of the tree calls (`DrawNode`) keeps the locals of its rare paths out of its own frame when they hold references (`Paint?`, a style struct): NativeAOT zeroes such locals on each call. V1c's first draw stage had a frame of 0x3C8 bytes, zeroed about 620 of them per node, and drew 1,000 boxes 30 µs slower; moving the rare paths to a `NoInlining` method that ordinary nodes do not call brought it to 4 µs. A single method for both paths did not help, since every node with a look still called it. Frames are compared with `dumpbin /disasm`: the `sub rsp` and the registers saved (PR #327).
- A design that wraps a user's callback on the frame path (running a handler in its scope) says how the wrapping allocates nothing. `Scope.Run(scope, () => handler(in e))` makes a closure per event, and a lambda cannot capture an `in` argument; V1d's first design said only "run it with `Scope.Run`", and the review found it. The input enters and exits the scope with internal `TryEnter` and `Exit` instead (PR #336).
- A frame's first call runs with cold caches, where a field it reads costs a miss and a field it only writes does not. State that only a slow path uses (the glyphs a frame sees for the first time) lives in an object of its own, and a table that is usually empty is emptied when it is next used, not read every frame to see whether it is. In T5, `Reset` reading the pending count took about 100 ns against 22 to 26 ns for writing it, `BeginFrame` emptying the refused glyphs 100 to 200 ns, and a frame's build stage was 1.2 µs longer than main's before the state moved (PR #228).
- A cache that each of many nodes holds keeps the keys it is looked up by, and the values a hit returns, in the cache's own object, not in its entries. In V1a the widths of a text leaf's line-break cache lived in the entries, and looking one up took 9.8 % of the samples of resizing 10,101 text leaves; with the widths and the sizes in the cache (`InlineArray`), a hit read no entry, and the resize with cache hits went from 1.89 to 2.66 ms to 1.66 to 1.92 ms (alternated 4 rounds, PR #301).
- A fast form with a limited range is checked against the range the design allows for the value before it goes in. W1 rounded rectangle edges by adding and taking away 1.5 × 2^23, exact only below 2^22 pixels, which a long list's virtual coordinates pass; the review found it. A rounding on the frame path is measured in the NativeAOT build: for the 1,000 rectangles of `frames` at 125 %, the display list took 32.1 to 32.7 µs with `MathF.Round` and 30.2 to 30.7 µs with SSE4.1's `ROUNDSS` (`Sse41.RoundToNearestIntegerScalar`), the same rounding, with `MathF.Round` as the scalar path and a test that compares them bit for bit (PR #236).
- A loop over children on the frame path reads only what it needs, and work for a part of the tree the change did not touch stays constant. In L2 a block copied each child's style before checking that the child was in the flow (a change in the absolute scene 21.6 µs, 10.4 µs after), and every relayout walked a subtree that was already hidden (a sibling's change with 10,100 hidden nodes 145 µs, 1.16 µs after; the review found it) (PR #125).
- A check per child for a feature that most children do not use reads a flag on the node, set when the style is set, and comes after the test that most children take. In L3, reading each absolutely positioned child's four minimums and maximums made a change in the absolute scene 5.2 % slower than main, and a flag (`LayoutState.HasKeywordLimits`) 0.7 %; checking a flex item's keyword limits before its base-size memo made one change with CSS's defaults 3.1 % slower, and after the memo 1.1 % (`layout-loop`, main and the branch alternated 12 times each, PR #134).
- A check on the frame path that only asks whether a large optional struct is set tests it where it lives; it does not return a copy to compare with `null`. K1a's first `PseudoStyles.Of` returned a `PaintStyle?` of about 108 bytes for `SetPseudoState` to test, and a hover change took 0.217 to 0.232 µs against main's 0.189 to 0.190 µs; `PseudoStyles.Has`, which tests each style in place, took 0.194 to 0.197 µs (PR #356).
- A `ref` into an arena is never held across an operation that can grow the arena (design §2.5, Invariant 12).
- `MathF.Min` and `MathF.Max` order NaN and -0, about 13 instructions each in NativeAOT. Where that order does not matter, a comparison (`a < b ? a : b`, one `minss`) is used: in R4c a group's bounds, widened four times a child, cost a child 2.4 ns less with it (PR #264).
- A comparison on the frame path does not use the generated `==` of a record struct: it goes through `EqualityComparer<T>` field by field and was not inlined, 19 % of the samples of a change in the absolute scene in L7. Compare the fields directly (2.22 to 1.98 µs, PR #138).
- The frame path does not read a buffer mapped for writing (`D3D11_MAP_WRITE_DISCARD`): it is write-combined and slow to read. R4c first moved the commands inside a layer after writing them, and the Draw stage took about 120 µs more for 1,000 rectangles; subtracting the layer's origin before writing made it main's again (PR #264).
- Explicit SIMD (`Vector128<T>`, `Vector<T>`) only where a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck, with a scalar path that gives the same result, and a test that compares them.

## Claims

A speed or memory improvement is claimed with numbers against a named baseline (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`). Not with a hunch, an IL reading, or a micro-benchmark of a different path.

```csharp
// BAD — allocates a closure, a delegate, an enumerator, and a list every frame
var dirty = nodes.Where(n => n.Dirty.SelfDirty != DirtyFlags.None && n.Id != skip).ToList();

// GOOD — no allocation in steady state
foreach (ref readonly var node in CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(nodes))
{
    if (node.Dirty.SelfDirty == DirtyFlags.None || node.Id == skip) continue;
    // ...
}
```
